{
  "id": "spark-int-arith",
  "engine": "spark",
  "engine_version": "4.3.0",
  "title": "Integer `+ - *` and unary `-`: raise under ANSI, wrap without",
  "verdict": "ANSI on: `overflow: ERROR` at every width; ANSI off: `SILENT`",
  "area": "integers",
  "options": [
    "overflow"
  ],
  "rows": [
    {
      "group": "int.arith",
      "option": "overflow",
      "types": [
        "i8",
        "i16",
        "i32",
        "i64"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "constructs": [
    "+",
    "-",
    "*",
    "unary -",
    "negative"
  ],
  "settings": {
    "spark.sql.ansi.enabled": [
      true,
      false
    ]
  },
  "description": [
    "Spark keeps the operands' width: `TINYINT + TINYINT` is a `TINYINT`, and so on up to `BIGINT`. With `spark.sql.ansi.enabled` on (the default since 4.0) `+`, `-`, `*` and unary `-` raise past the width (`BINARY_ARITHMETIC_OVERFLOW` for `TINYINT` and `SMALLINT`, `ARITHMETIC_OVERFLOW` for `INT` and `BIGINT`). With it off they wrap in two's complement: `127Y + 1Y` is -128, `-(-2⁶³)` is -2⁶³.",
    "`try_add`, `try_subtract` and `try_multiply` give NULL on overflow in either mode (spark-try-variants). An integer literal without a suffix is an `INT`, so `2147483647 + 1` raises under ANSI (spark-literal-types)."
  ],
  "reference": "`add`, `subtract`, `multiply`, `negate` with `overflow: ERROR` when the plan omits it (#202); `SILENT` when the plan asks. Spark under ANSI gives `ERROR` natively at every width; without ANSI it gives `SILENT`.",
  "status": "no-finding",
  "evidence": "probe",
